+# DOJ's 2014 routing entry for September 11 Solicitor General telephone records
+
+Public derivative of the November 10, 2026 source review · TEL01, TEL03–TEL04 / AUD016-A1 / TC05–TC06. A Department of Justice Office of Information Policy (OIP) log supplies a case number for the search for the receiving office's telephone records. The request description specifies the Office of the Solicitor General (OSG) and September 11, 2001. This is a later administrative record about a request for records. The historical telephone records and the receiving office's response are still unacquired.
+
+## Exact entry and source comparison
+
+| Field | Published entry |
+| --- | --- |
+| Request number | `FOIA-2014-01777` |
+| Office | `OIP` |
+| Date closed | `3/12/14` |
+| Summary | “Telephone records from the Office of the Solicitor General on September 11, 2001” |
+| Disposition | “Referred requester and/or request to more appropriate entity (OSG)” |
+| Exemption fields | Both blank |
+
+The current and legacy DOJ PDF URLs returned identical 469,380-byte files. The relevant row is on printed and PDF p. 3, sorted extraction lines 43–45. The March CSV contains the same seven fields at physical line 35, the 34th data record after the header. The subject and disposition in the table preserve the CSV text exactly. The page image confirms that the referral wording belongs to 01777 and that the exemption cells are blank. A separate row on p. 3 and CSV line 30, `FOIA-2014-01901`, shows `Full Grant` for a federal FOIA contact-list request. The case numbers and dispositions remain distinct. [PDF p. 3](https://www.justice.gov/oip/docs/closed-foia-log-mar2014.pdf#page=3) · [Legacy PDF p. 3](https://www.justice.gov/sites/default/files/oip/legacy/2014/07/23/closed-foia-log-mar2014.pdf#page=3) · [CSV](https://www.justice.gov/oip/docs/closed-foia-log-mar2014.csv)
+
+The case row records OIP's closure and uses “requester and/or request.” It leaves open which form of routing occurred, whether OSG received the request, whether either office located telephone records and what OSG determined or released. OIP's FOIA library describes logs covering a listed set of offices and notes that a log can refer readers to another office. OSG has its own FOIA and electronic reading-room pages. OIP's 2008 routing guidance describes forwarding misdirected requests within a department. The published guidance supplies no case-specific dispatch or receipt date. [OIP library, log section](https://www.justice.gov/oip/available-documents-oip) · [OSG electronic reading room](https://www.justice.gov/osg/electronic-reading-room) · [OIP routing guidance](https://www.justice.gov/oip/blog/foia-post-2008-oip-guidance-new-requirement-route-misdirected-foia-requests)
+
+## Scope tested
+
+- All 12 calendar-2014 CSVs were retrieved from DOJ's Older FOIA Logs index and parsed as seven-field records. A screen across 1,020 data rows for the exact ID and a bounded telephone/phone plus Solicitor General subject pattern yielded only the March row. This is an OIP log screen. Any OSG-only tracking number, unpublished disposition or differently worded row remains possible. [Index](https://www.justice.gov/oip/older-foia-logs)
+- The OSG FOIA and electronic reading-room landing pages returned no match for the request ID or the phrase `telephone records`. The screen did not enumerate separate agency systems or archives. The FY 2014 archive URL returned HTTP 200 with an HTML browser-verification interstitial. No annual-report contents were obtained from that response.
+- The PDF variants are two deliveries of the same bytes. The CSV is a separate representation of the OIP log. Neither the cross-format match nor OIP's procedural guidance independently corroborates an original call, recipient, transfer leg or search outcome.
+
+## Records still needed
+
+1. Find the original request, March 12 OIP disposition letter and any OIP-to-OSG transmittal and receipt. Then seek an OSG intake number, search description, signed response and a previously released record list or actual public packet. The log wording leaves both the transfer and the response unverified.
+2. Preserve the existing connected and unconnected accounts for the reported AA77 event and the distinct reported collect/direct office legs. The prior telephone review preserves the FBI September 20 report, the later DOJ briefing and stored court display separately. Test an office leg against the carrier or operator event only if identifiers, service, clock and route actually join. Keep the complete Request 14 package, native carrier and operator rows, office incoming/transfer record and FBI usage-report attachments open. [Prior source and conflict review](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/2d73a93d12276caae2b6029eff5c504bfdb8ff3b/investigation/broader-evidence/telephone-zero-entry-context-verified-2026-11-02/README.md)
+
+This unit verified a receiving-office lead and its exact source fields. No released telephone packet or authenticated call-level join was found in the sources read.
